.TH XMQ 1
.SH NAME
xmq \- Command-line xmq/htmq/xml/html/json/yaml processor
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBxmq\fP [global-options] <input> [commands-and-options ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION

The xmq tool can extract data from, and convert between, xmq, htmq, xml,
html, json and yaml documents.  It can pretty print, compact, colorize,
tokenize, transform (with xslq), validate against a schema and perform
other operations on the content of the document.

xmq can also extract data from any other text format for which an
invisible xml (ixml) grammar has been written, and download ready-made
ixml grammars and xsl transforms from the libxmq library at
https://libxmq.org/library.

If no commands are specified, xmq reads the input and prints it as xmq.
When the output goes to a terminal the document is rendered with color;
when it is piped you get the same pretty-printed xmq without color.
The most common form of use is therefore simply:

.RS
\fBxmq some_file.xml\fP
.RE

Read from standard input when the input is the single character \fB\-\fP
(or when no input file is given and stdin is a pipe).

Command and option ordering matters: there are two kinds of options.
\fIGlobal options\fP (the ones in the next section) are recognized before
any input or command, and \fIper-command options\fP (most of the ones
documented in the "OPTIONS" section) must be placed immediately after the
command they apply to, e.g. \fBxmq pom.xml to-json \-p\fP.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS

Options in this section are recognized before the input file and the
commands.  The other options, described in the ``OPTIONS'' section
below, must be placed after the command they apply to.

.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help.

.TP
.BR \-z " (empty dom)"
Start with an empty DOM; any input file is ignored.  Combine with
\fBadd\fP to build a document from scratch.

.TP
.BR \-y " (yes)"
Automatically answer yes to any questions (e.g. about downloading
library files).

.TP
.BR \-i " (in-is-content)"
The next argument is the content to be parsed, not a file name, e.g.
\fBxmq \-i 'a=2' render-terminal\fP .

.TP
.BR \-\-xmq ", " \-\-xml", " \-\-html", " \-\-ixml", " \-\-json", " \-\-yaml", " \-\-clines ",
" \-\-text", " \-\-lines
Force the input content type instead of auto-detecting it.
Normally the content type is auto-detected from the file extension (when
a file is given) or the content itself (when reading from stdin).

.TP
.BR \-\-lines
Treat each input line as a separate document (for formats like
json-lines where each line is a standalone object).

.TP
.BR \-\-root=NAME
When a document has no (single) root element (e.g. a list of json
objects), wrap it in a synthesized root element with the given name.

.TP
.BR \-\-trim=none|heuristic|exact
Control how whitespace is trimmed from the input.
.br
.BR none : " do not trim"
.br
.BR heuristic " use heuristics"
.br
.BR exact : " not implemented yet; xmq warns and exits with status 1"

.TP
.BR \-\-no-merge
Do not merge adjacent text nodes during parsing.

.TP
.BR \-\-force-download
Always download library files, even if a cached copy exists.

.TP
.BR \-\-ixml=NAME
Load an invisible xml grammar (named \fBN.ixml\fP, \fBN.xml\fP or
\fBN.xmq\fP, possibly from the library).
The grammar defines how the input text is parsed.
Use with a .ixml/.xml/.xmq grammar file:
.br
.RS
\fBxmq input.txt \-\-ixml=mygrammar.ixml to-json\fP
.RE

.TP
.BR \-\-ixml-all-parses
Report all parses matched by the ixml grammar, not just the first.

.TP
.BR \-\-ixml-try-to-recover
Try to recover a parse even if the first attempt fails.

.TP
.BR \-\-ixml-fail-silent
Do not warn if an ixml parse fails.

.TP
.BR \-\-ixml-engine=NAME
Use the named ixml engine implementation.
Can also be set with the \fBXMQ_IXML_ENGINE\fP environment variable.

.TP
.BR \-\-xml-of-ixml
Output the xml representation of the ixml parse result (debugging aid).

.TP
.BR \-\-list-library
Print the library index (ixml grammars and xsl transforms) available
from https://libxmq.org/library.

.TP
.BR \-\-trace[=filter] ", " \-\-debug[=filter] ", " \-\-verbose[=filter]
Enable and optionally filter trace/debug/verbose diagnostic output.

.TP
.BR \-lx ", " \-\-log-xmq
Write log output in xmq format.

.TP
.BR \-\-check-bg-dark-light
Report whether the current terminal is configured for dark or light
background, then exit with status 0 if the mode is mono (no color),
1 if it is dark and 2 if it is light.

.TP
.BR \-\-check-truecolor
Check whether the current terminal supports truecolor; exit 0 if it
does, 1 otherwise.

.TP
.BR \-\-version
Print the version and exit.

.TP
.BR \-\-license
Print the license and exit.
.SH COMMANDS

The commands are executed in order.  Most document-modifying commands
(add, delete, replace, ...) must come before an output command (to-*,
render-*, tokenize, save-to, page, browse, print).
.SS OUTPUT COMMANDS

.TP
.BR "to-xmq" " [options]"
Write the document out as xmq.  This is the default output format when
no output command is given.

.TP
.BR "to-htmq" " [options]"
Write the document out as htmq (a htmq subset of xmq).
Output is pretty printed by default; use \fB\-c\fP for the compact
one-line form.

.TP
.BR "to-xml" " [options]"
Write the document out as xml.
The \fB<?xml ...?>\fP declaration is included by default; use
\fB\-o\fP (or \fB\-\-omit-decl\fP) to suppress it.

.TP
.BR "to-html" " [options]"
Write the document out as html (no declaration emitted).
Output is compact by default; use \fB\-p\fP for pretty printing.

.TP
.BR "to-json" " [options]"
Write the document out as json.
Output is compact by default; use \fB\-p\fP for a pretty-printed form.

.TP
.BR "to-yaml" " [options]"
Write the document out as yaml.

.TP
.BR "to-text" " [options]"
Write the plain text content of the document.

.TP
.BR "to-clines" " [options]"
Write the document as c-lines: one path-based key=value pair per line
with c-style quoting, e.g. \fB/_/a/@name="value"\fP or
\fB/_/list/_="one"\fP .

.TP
.BR "render-terminal" " [options]"
Render the document formatted for a terminal and print it.
This is the default rendering when the output goes to a tty.
This command honors the \fB\-\-theme=\fP and \fB\-\-bg=\fP options (see
THEME AND COLORS below).

.TP
.BR "render-html" " [options]"
Render the document as a self-contained html page with embedded css
(both a light and a dark color scheme are included and the browser
picks the right one with \fBprefers-color-scheme\fP).

.TP
.BR "render-tex" " [options]"
Render the document as a LaTeX file.

.TP
.BR "tokenize " "[\-\-type=terminal|html|tex|debugtokens|debugcontent|location]"
Tokenize the input without building a DOM and print the tokens.
.br
.RS
.nf
\-\-type=terminal     print colored tokens to the terminal
\-\-type=html         print tokens annotated with <xmqEK>... tags
\-\-type=tex          print tokens with LaTeX markup
\-\-type=debugtokens  print each token on one line
\-\-type=debugcontent print token contents with positions
\-\-type=location     print token positions
.fi
.RE
.SS DOCUMENT MODIFICATION COMMANDS

.TP
.BR "add" " xmq-content"
Parse xmq-content as xmq and append it to the document as new
top-level content (a sibling after the document's last element).

.TP
.BR "add-root" " NAME"
Wrap the (possibly rootless) document in a new root element with the
given name.

.TP
.BR "delete" " [xpath-expression]"
Remove the node(s) matching the xpath expression.
A non-matching expression leaves the document unchanged.
Invoked without an expression the document is passed through unmodified.

.TP
.BR "select" " xpath-expression"
Keep only the node(s) matching the xpath expression.

.TP
.BR "replace" " xpath=value"
Replace the node(s) matching the xpath expression with the xmq content
given after the \fB=\fP.

.TP
.BR "replace-entity" " ENTITY new-content"
Set the content of the named entity to the given xmq content (the
entity is created if it does not already exist).
.TP
.BR \-\-with-file
The second argument is a file, its parsed content (xmq, xml, html, json...)
is the replacement: \fBreplace-entity --with-file ENTITY file\fP
.TP
.BR \-\-with-text-file
The second argument is a file, its content is the replacement as plain text:
\fBreplace-entity --with-text-file ENTITY file\fP

.TP
.BR "delete-entity" " [ENTITY]"
Remove the entity named ENTITY (both its definition and any
references).
Invoked without a name it is a harmless no-op.

.TP
.BR "substitute-entity" " [ENTITY]"
Resolve entity references in the document: a reference to ENTITY (e.g.
\fB%name%\fP) is replaced with the content of that entity's definition,
and character references (\fI&#65;\fP, \fI&#x41;\fP) are expanded to the character they
denote.  If no ENTITY is given, all entity references are resolved.
Note that when the output format is xmq, references that survive in the
document are still written on output as \fB%name%\fP.

.TP
.BR substitute-char-entities
Like \fBsubstitute-entity\fP but only expands character references
(\fI&#65;\fP and \fI&#x41;\fP) to the characters they denote.

.TP
.BR heu
Using a heuristic, rename unnamed elements (elements named \fB_\fP)
after their parent: a child of \fIbranches\fP becomes \fIbranch\fP,
a child of \fIsteps\fP becomes \fIstep\fP, and so on (the singular
form of the parent's name).
An unnamed root is named after the \fB\-\-root=NAME\fP global option
(placed before the input file) when given, otherwise after the input
file name (with the extension stripped).
The input must be a valid xmq (or json) document.
.SS DATA EXTRACTION COMMANDS

.TP
.BR "for-each" " xpath-expression \-\-shell=\(dqcommand\(dq"
For each node matching the xpath expression, xmq looks for
\fB${name}\fP occurrences in the command, sets a shell environment
variable for each one (with \fB.\fP meaning the node's text, \fB..\fP
meaning the node's name, and any other name meaning that child element's
text), and runs the command under \fB/bin/sh\fP .
The command is executed once per matched node.

.TP
.BR statistics
Print statistics about the document (size, number of elements, etc.).
This is an output command; it produces output on the terminal.
.SS LOAD, VALIDATE AND TRANSFORM COMMANDS

.TP
.BR "load" " file [options]"
Read and parse another file (format auto-detected; .yml/.yaml files
are parsed as yaml; an ixml grammar can be applied with the
\fB\-\-ixml=\fP global option) and merge the result into the document.
.TP
.BR \-\-alias=NAME
Rename the loaded document's root element to NAME before merging.

.TP
.BR "validate" " schema-file.xsd [options]"
Validate the document against the given xml schema (xsxsl).
Exits non-zero if validation fails.
.TP
.BR \-\-silent
Only print the error, not the document.

.TP
.BR "transform" " xslfile.xslq|library_name [options]"
Apply the given xsl transform (in xslq format) to the document.
The transform can be a local file or a library name that will be
downloaded and cached from https://libxmq.org/library.
.TP
.BR \-\-param=NAME=VALUE
Set a numeric parameter in the transform.
.TP
.BR \-\-stringparam=NAME=VALUE
Set a string parameter in the transform.
.SS QUOTE / UNQUOTE

.TP
.BR "quote-c" " [options]"
Output the document content as a C-quoted string literal (with quotes
and escapes), suitable for embedding in C source.
.TP
.BR \-\-add-nl
Append a trailing newline escape sequence to the quoted output.

.TP
.BR "unquote-c" " [options]"
Interpret the input as a C string literal, decode its escapes and print
the resulting plain content.
.TP
.BR \-\-add-nl
Append a trailing newline to the output.
.SS OUTPUT DESTINATION COMMANDS

.TP
.BR print
Print the output (the default if no output destination is given).

.TP
.BR no-output
Consume the document without producing any output at all.

.TP
.BR "page" " (pa)"
Page the output in a built-in pager (requires a terminal as output;
xmq reports an error and exits if stdout is not a tty).
Keys:
.nf
    q/esc/q       quit
    space/pagedown next page
    pageup        previous page
    down/up arrow next/previous line
    enter         next line
    <             jump to start
    >             jump to end
.fi

.TP
.BR "browse" " (br)"
Open the rendered html in the default browser.

.TP
.BR "save-to" " filename"
Save the output to a file.
.SS HELP

.TP
.BR "help" " [command]"
Print the help text, optionally for a specific command.
.SH "OPTIONS FOR to-*/render-*/tokenize"
The options in this section are \fIper-command\fP options: they must be
placed after the command they apply to, and each is only accepted by the
commands listed.

.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pretty
(to-*/render-*) Pretty-print the output.  This is the default for
to-xmq/to-htmq/to-yaml, while to-json and to-html are compact by
default.

.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-compact
(to-*/render-*) Write the output as a single line.

.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no-final-nl
(to-*/render-*) Do not write a trailing newline.

.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-omit-decl
(to-xml only) Omit the leading \fB<?xml ...?>\fP declaration line.

.TP
.BR \-\-indent=NUM
(to-xmq, to-htmq, to-yaml, render-*) Use NUM spaces for each indent
level (default 4 for xmq/htmq, 2 for json/yaml when pretty-printed).

.TP
.BR \-\-escape-newlines
(to-xmq, to-htmq, to-yaml, render-*) Escape newlines inside quoted
values.

.TP
.BR \-\-escape-tabs
(to-xmq, to-htmq, to-yaml, render-*) Escape tab characters.

.TP
.BR \-\-escape-non-7bit
(to-xmq, to-htmq, to-yaml, render-*) Escape all non-7-bit (i.e.
non-ASCII) characters.

.TP
.BR \-\-prefer-double-quotes
(to-xmq, to-htmq, to-yaml, render-*) Produce double-quoted strings in
the output.

.TP
.BR \-\-add-nl
(quote-c/unquote-c) Append a trailing newline escape to the quoted
output.

.TP
.BR \-\-class=NAME
(render-*) Use the given css class name instead of the default.

.TP
.BR \-\-id=NAME
(render-*) Add the given id to the rendered element.

.TP
.BR \-\-nostyle
(render-*) Do not include any preamble/style (the render is \fIraw\fP).

.TP
.BR \-\-onlystyle
(render-*) Output only the preamble/style block (the css/latex
header), not the content.

.TP
.BR \-\-theme=light|dark|SPEC
(render-*) Force the light or dark theme, or supply a custom theme spec
(see THEME AND COLORS below).

.TP
.BR \-\-bg=light|dark|mono
(render-*) Force the background to be light, dark, or turn all color
off (mono).
Normally the background is auto-detected from the environment.
An unknown value is reported and xmq exits with status 1.

.TP
.BR \-\-use-cr=STR
(render-*) Use STR as the explicit carriage-return rendering.

.TP
.BR \-\-use-es=STR
(render-*) Use STR as the explicit-space rendering.

.TP
.BR \-\-use-et=STR
(render-*) Use STR as the explicit-tab rendering.

.TP
.BR \-\-use-is=STR
(render-*) Use STR as the explicit-indentation-space rendering.

.TP
.BR \-\-use-nl=STR
(render-*) Use STR as the explicit-newline rendering.

.TP
.BR \-\-type=terminal|html|tex|debugtokens|debugcontent|location
(tokenize) Select the token output format (see the \fBtokenize\fP
entry above).
.SH THEME AND COLORS

Color is used by \fIrender-terminal\fP, \fIto-xmq\fP, \fIto-htmq\fP,
\fIrender-html\fP and \fIrender-tex\fP .  When the output goes to a
terminal the document is rendered in color; when it is piped the
output is plain text, unless you explicitly force a dark or light
mode with \fB\-\-bg=dark\fP or \fB\-\-bg=light\fP (a render-* option,
placed after the command), in which case color escapes are emitted
even when piping.  All color can be turned off explicitly with
\fB\-\-bg=mono\fP or \fBXMQ_BG=mono\fP .
The background (dark or light) is auto-detected from the environment
unless overridden with \fB\-\-bg=\fP or the \fBXMQ_BG\fP environment
variable (\fBlight\fP, \fBdark\fP or \fBmono\fP).

The default theme is built into xmq.  You can define and customize
themes with the \fB\-\-theme=\fP option (render-* commands) or the
\fBXMQ_THEME\fP environment variable, which applies to all rendering.
A theme spec has the form:

.RS
.nf
dark+COLOR=#RRGGBB[_B][_U]:COLOR=#RRGGBB[...],light+COLOR=#RRGGBB[...]
.fi
.RE

where each named color can be overridden, and unprefixed names apply to
both the dark and light variants.  \fB_B\fP requests bold and \fB_U\fP
underline for that color.
The 15 color names and their roles are:

.RS
.TP
.B C
comments
.TP
.B Q
quotes
.TP
.B E
entities
.TP
.B NS
namespaces
.TP
.B EN
element names (not keys)
.TP
.B EK
element keys
.TP
.B EKV
element key-values
.TP
.B AK
attribute keys
.TP
.B AKV
attribute key-values
.TP
.B CP
composite parentheses
.TP
.B NSD
namespace declarations
.TP
.B UW
unicode whitespace
.TP
.B XSL
namespace label xsl
.TP
.B FG
foreground (text) color
.TP
.B BG
background color
.RE

The default dark+light theme (for reference) is:

.RS
.nf
XMQ_THEME=dark+C=#2aa1b3:Q=#26a269_B:E=#c061cb:NS=#a9a9a9:EN=#ff8c00:EK=#88b4f7:EKV=#26a269_B:AK=#88b4f7:AKV=#6196ec:CP=#c061cb:NSD=#2aa1b3:UW=#880000_U:XSL=#c061cb,light+C=#2aa1b3:Q=#26a269_B:E=#c061cb:NS=#696969:EN=#a86c00:EK=#0060fd:EKV=#26a269_B:AK=#0060fd:AKV=#12488c:CP=#c061cb:NSD=#1a91a3:UW=#880000_U:XSL=#c061cb
.fi
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR XMQ_BG
Force the background color mode: \fBlight\fP, \fBdark\fP or \fBmono\fP .

.TP
.BR XMQ_THEME
Theme spec (see THEME AND COLORS above), equivalent to
\fB\-\-theme=\fP but applies to the whole invocation.

.TP
.BR XMQ_TERM
Set the terminal color depth: \fBXMQ_TERM=truecolor\fP forces 24-bit
(truecolor) rendering; any other non-empty value forces the 256-color
mode.

.TP
.BR XMQ_IXML_ENGINE
Select the ixml engine to use (same as \fB\-\-ixml-engine=\fP).

.TP
.BR COLORFGBG
Consulted when the terminal background cannot be auto-detected: if the
value ends in \fB;0\fP the background is assumed to be dark.
.SH FILES
.TP
.BI $HOME/.local/share/xmq/
Directory where downloaded library files (ixml grammars, xsl
transforms) are cached.
.TP
.BI /usr/local/share/xmq/
System-wide directory for locally installed library files.
.SH EXAMPLES

.TP
Pretty print:
.RS
.nf
$ xmq pom.xml
$ xmq pom.xml render-terminal
.fi
.RE
.TP
Convert between formats:
.RS
.nf
$ xmq pom.xml to-json > pom.json
$ xmq pom.json to-html br
$ xmq pom.xml to-html > pom.html
$ xmq pom.xml to-json save-to pom.json
$ xmq pom.xml to-text
$ xmq pom.yml to-htmq -p
.fi
.RE
.TP
Tokenize:
.RS
.nf
$ xmq pom.xml tokenize \-\-type=html
$ xmq pom.xml tokenize \-\-type=debugcontent
.fi
.RE
.TP
Extract data with xpath / for-each:
.RS
.nf
$ xmq pom.xml select //dependency save-to deps.xmq
$ xmq pom.xml for-each //dependency \-\-shell='echo \&"${artifactId} \&-${version}"'
$ xmq pom.xml for-each //dependency \-\-shell='echo \&"${..} says ${.}"'
.fi
.RE
.TP
Transform with xslq:
.RS
.nf
$ xmq pom.xml transform web-view.xslq to-html > web.html
$ xmq data.json transform xsl:data/table-to-web br
$ xmq pom.xml transform mvn \-\-stringparam=version=1.2.3 to-html
.fi
.RE
.TP
Build a document from scratch:
.RS
.nf
$ xmq \-z add name=Fred add age=42 add-root person to-xml
$ xmq \-z add a=1 add b=2 to-json -p
.fi
.RE
.TP
Validate:
.RS
.nf
$ xmq data.xml validate data.xsd
$ xmq data.json validate schema.json \-\-silent
.fi
.RE
.TP
Use an invisible xml grammar:
.RS
.nf
$ xmq input.txt \-\-ixml=mygrammar.ixml to-json
$ xmq input.csv \-\-ixml=csv xsl:data/table-to-web br
.fi
.RE
.TP
Load one document into another:
.RS
.nf
$ xmq base.pom.xml load extra.xmq to-html
$ xmq base.pom.xml load \-\-alias=EXTRA extra.xmq to-html
.fi
.RE
.TP
Statistics:
.RS
.nf
$ xmq pom.xml statistics
.fi
.RE
.TP
Quote/unquote for C:
.RS
.nf
$ xmq \-i 'hello, "world"' quote-c
$ echo \-n \(dqhello \\\\(cq\(dq | xmq \- unquote-c
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
\fBxmq\fP exits with status 0 on success and with a non-zero status on
error (parse errors, failed validation, etc.).
The \fBvalidate\fP command exits non-zero when the document does not
validate against the schema.
The \fB\-\-trim=exact\fP option is not implemented: xmq prints a warning
and exits with status 1.
The two check options have a special status scheme:
\fB\-\-check-bg-dark-light\fP exits 0 for mono (no color), 1 for a dark
background and 2 for a light background; \fB\-\-check-truecolor\fP exits
0 if the terminal supports truecolor and 1 otherwise.
